        "title": "Primordium",
        "original": {
          "source": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Root_primordia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=original",
          "width": 3280,
          "height": 2460
        },
        "pageimage": "Root_primordia.JPG",
        "extract": "A primordium (; pl.: primordia; synonym: anlage), in embryology, is an organ or tissue in its earliest recognizable stage of development.  Cells of the primordium are called primordial cells. A primordium is the simplest set of cells capable of triggering growth of the would-be organ and the initial foundation from which an organ is able to grow. In flowering plants, a floral primordium gives rise to a flower.\nAlthough it is a frequently used term in plant biology, the word is used in describing the biology of all multicellular organisms (for example: a tooth primordium in animals, a leaf primordium in plants or a sporophore primordium in fungi.)",
        "pageprops": {
          "page_image_free": "Root_primordia.JPG",
          "wikibase-shortdesc": "Organ in the earliest recognizable stage of embryonic development",
          "wikibase_item": "Q557677"
        },
